2}^{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-346204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d6317e4b24f953776249e6db2b9d49942736e1c4f5f9a98ef66656a5d14914f"} {"question" "q-346204" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-346204" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-346204" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-346204" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73178" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d6317e4b24f953776249e6db2b9d49942736e1c4f5f9a98ef66656a5d14914f"} {"question" "q-73178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$"] ["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$"] ["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$"] ["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$"] ["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is ${\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x},$ the lower bound is $a=4$ and the upper bound is $b=9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{9-4}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{9}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(4+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d6317e4b24f953776249e6db2b9d49942736e1c4f5f9a98ef66656a5d14914f"} {"question" "q-113941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{9+\\frac{4k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{4+\\frac{5k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113927" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d6317e4b24f953776249e6db2b9d49942736e1c4f5f9a98ef66656a5d14914f"} {"question" "q-113927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{4k/n}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{k/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-113927" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{e}^{2k/n}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-49192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is ${\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-49192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=5{x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=3$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{4}5{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(3+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-49192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d6317e4b24f953776249e6db2b9d49942736e1c4f5f9a98ef66656a5d14914f"} {"question" "q-49192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-49192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(1+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(4+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-49192" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[5{(3+\\frac{i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=2$ and the upper bound is $b=6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{6-2}{n}=\\frac{4}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(2+j\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73182" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d6317e4b24f953776249e6db2b9d49942736e1c4f5f9a98ef66656a5d14914f"} {"question" "q-73182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[2\\sqrt{6+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{4j}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] ["math" "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-73182" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2+\\frac{4j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-315830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-315830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=9{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}9{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{3i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[9{(1+\\frac{3i}{n})}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-315830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d6317e4b24f953776249e6db2b9d49942736e1c4f5f9a98ef66656a5d14914f"} {"question" "q-315830" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "q-315830" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+\\frac{3i}{n}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/state.json" "file_sha256" "71e4e23ce6ffc25942b10962382cc682087d08e505f08efd8deda2bb5d09496f"} {"question" "e-405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/assets/f02616ea8f44865a727bec3affe596503dbadacafb113acad6e5f755218b4ff5.png)\n\nFor the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/example-405.json" "file_sha256" "5cc0c7c8ae25818cd66599ac9787c6753daa2cb8dede4809f57925bd97ec1c01"} {"question" "e-405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/example-405.json" "file_sha256" "5cc0c7c8ae25818cd66599ac9787c6753daa2cb8dede4809f57925bd97ec1c01"} {"question" "e-406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/example-406.json" "file_sha256" "3e2732c4f1f8cfdca861a6ec2a08fabc97dba3b2b1fe83dc8364e66178977a73"} {"question" "e-406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = 4-x,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(4-(1+\\frac{2k}{n}))\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(3-\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/example-406.json" "file_sha256" "3e2732c4f1f8cfdca861a6ec2a08fabc97dba3b2b1fe83dc8364e66178977a73"} {"question" "e-407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/example-407.json" "file_sha256" "d1101df94ca9b4e9d095758a2774522b0bee4d9e70729a4c7cea11513b7d5893"} {"question" "e-407" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}$ and $f(x_k) = x_{k}^2.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)= x^2$ and $b = 8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/example-407.json" "file_sha256" "d1101df94ca9b4e9d095758a2774522b0bee4d9e70729a4c7cea11513b7d5893"} {"question" "e-408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x$ as the limit of a right Riemann sum." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/example-408.json" "file_sha256" "121c007fef9b05434d1ddfae5d8f898dcf4c696f28f8e824f164e7c6af7d2669"} {"question" "e-408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = x^2,$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/example-408.json" "file_sha256" "121c007fef9b05434d1ddfae5d8f898dcf4c696f28f8e824f164e7c6af7d2669"} {"question" "q-73178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "fe4a9adc5ab4b743ec838eda1cb8811fab24640d62c5bebb737718c1cb7ccc90" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "The left Riemann sum has a=-π/4 and Δx=π/(2n), so b=a+nΔx=π/4. Its sample points are x_l=a+lΔx, and the summand evaluates sec²(x_l), establishing f(x)=sec²x. This matches original option 1, the authentic worked solution, and saved Correct grading. This is a derived mathematical judgment, not a direct answer-key capture. The stored wording expresses precisely the same function and endpoint." "bindings" {"problem" "643d28c1f86f492548c53b077fb53f6b7b37483693ef751249341b1e684db06b" "worked_solution" "298ac63bab1f570b93f6b76bef8198445398ac02ad9e44c4e547e49e21ba3943" "original_field" "ad4fa6f724beae1bd35cacf54c49fdfdc63754c72927c498085f47ecc220677e" "reviewed_field" "e47860ebf57e6e132f259cd86f35c65f09ea0c5222b220296a7dcd8e83a3ed09" "raw_widget" "97b014478a4ce7c18eede39aa9c2992a68761e65e724cd291c8ce6035d9e2834"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-73178-before.png" "sha256" "98755777ca7451d5ef0779e299813a7c0e897f8c46f3c2917602b09b0f4750bb"} {"path" "history-q-73178.png" "sha256" "e2e071c3952a57278f08cce1e7e2cd370c662af1ee41ccac66aedb2ffe7fff5e"} {"path" "q-73178-after.json" "sha256" "85def7730475269a98f3159a74c0644d4241c0c1d8801261e737ac3e15722286"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "2b6aca6b16efce2a191c96d6948e3917c857932d96a0480c496c75b3a9a8f043"}]} {"question" "q-73178" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$"] ["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$"] ["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$"] ["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$"] ["text" "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080406/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "fe4a9adc5ab4b743ec838eda1cb8811fab24640d62c5bebb737718c1cb7ccc90" "rationale" "The left Riemann sum has a=-π/4 and Δx=π/(2n), so b=a+nΔx=π/4. Its sample points are x_l=a+lΔx, and the summand evaluates sec²(x_l), establishing f(x)=sec²x. This matches original option 1, the authentic worked solution, and saved Correct grading. This is a derived mathematical judgment, not a direct answer-key capture. The stored wording expresses precisely the same function and endpoint."}] "usage" {"q-113941" [] "q-346180" [] "q-49189" [] "e-406" [] "e-405" [] "q-30273" [] "q-30265" [] "e-408" [] "q-113927" [] "e-407" [] "q-73178" [] "q-315830" [] "q-113886" []} "basis" 2829 "retractions" [[17592186097129 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/q-30265.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x) =-x^{3} + 2x + 1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?"] [17592186097130 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/q-30273.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x) = x^{4} - 4x^{2} + 3$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?"] [17592186260600 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+l\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{l}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{l}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186260600 :question/answer-fields 17592186274759] [17592186257007 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4i}{n}-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{4i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{\\frac{i}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{8}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186257007 :question/answer-fields 17592186274289] [17592186261476 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$"] [17592186261476 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"] [17592186261476 :question/answer-fields 17592186274961] [17592186257022 :question/answer-fields 17592186274294] [17592186257016 :question/answer-fields 17592186274292] [17592186096354 :question/problem "For the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1086/Source/Images/0cc7c2b91797e6c24577ac66cfd8e215.png)"] [17592186096354 :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x^3+2\n$$\n\nand the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1$.\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of\n\n$$\nf(x) = -2x^3+2\n$$\n\nbetween the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$"] [17592186096355 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nthe function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = 4-x\n$$\n\nthe lower bound is $a=1$, and the upper bound is $b=3$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{3}(4 - x)dx &= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)f(x_{k})Δx \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[f(1 + k \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[f(1 + \\frac{2k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(4 - (1 + \\frac{2k}{n})) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 0)^(n - 1)[(3 - \\frac{2k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186096356 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b$, given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right]$."] [17592186096356 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3$.\n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nx_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf(x_k) = x_{k}^2\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\nf(x)= x^2\n$$\n\nand $b = 8$."] [17592186096357 :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx_k = a + k\\Delta x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nthe function is\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nthe lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{1}^{2}x^{2}dx &= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)f(x_{k})Δx \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[f(1 + k \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[f(1 + \\frac{k}{n}) \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)∑_(k = 1)^(n)[(1 + \\frac{k}{n})^{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186095137 17592186095138 17592186095139 17592186095140 17592186095141 17592186095143 17592186095144 17592186095145 17592186095146 17592186095147 17592186253122 17592186253125 17592186253126 17592186253128 17592186270067 17592186270068 17592186270069 17592186270070 17592186270071 17592186270082 17592186270083 17592186270084 17592186270085 17592186270086 17592186270092 17592186270093 17592186270094 17592186270095 17592186270096 17592186272395 17592186272396 17592186272397 17592186272398 17592186272399 17592186273387 17592186273388 17592186273389 17592186273390 17592186273391] "immutable_fields" [[17592186095136 :answer-field/correct] [17592186095136 :answer-field/id] [17592186095136 :answer-field/key] [17592186095136 :answer-field/type] [17592186095142 :answer-field/correct] [17592186095142 :answer-field/id] [17592186095142 :answer-field/key] [17592186095142 :answer-field/type] [17592186253121 :answer-field/correct] [17592186253121 :answer-field/id] [17592186253121 :answer-field/key] [17592186253121 :answer-field/type] [17592186253124 :answer-field/correct] [17592186253124 :answer-field/id] [17592186253124 :answer-field/key] [17592186253124 :answer-field/type] [17592186253127 :answer-field/correct] [17592186253127 :answer-field/id] [17592186253127 :answer-field/key] [17592186253127 :answer-field/type] [17592186274289 :answer-field/correct] [17592186274289 :answer-field/id] [17592186274289 :answer-field/key] [17592186274289 :answer-field/type] [17592186274292 :answer-field/correct] [17592186274292 :answer-field/id] [17592186274292 :answer-field/key] [17592186274292 :answer-field/type] [17592186274294 :answer-field/correct] [17592186274294 :answer-field/id] [17592186274294 :answer-field/key] [17592186274294 :answer-field/type] [17592186274759 :answer-field/correct] [17592186274759 :answer-field/id] [17592186274759 :answer-field/key] [17592186274759 :answer-field/type] [17592186274961 :answer-field/correct] [17592186274961 :answer-field/id] [1759218